    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d815">

                <pb facs="0827" n="513" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg513"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.731/56</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-10-30">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">October 30, 1914</hi>.</date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 4:45 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>944. Your 313, fourteenth instant. Sir Edward Grey has made further inquiries
                    regarding censoring of commercial cables and informs me that as a general rule
                    the senders are not informed of the stoppage of such messages and that exactly
                    same rules are applied in this respect to British and American senders.</p>
                <closer>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
